Now, update idx_1d72ac7c your main.tf file to use these variables. You reference a variable using the
syntax var.<variable_name>:
main.tf
terraform {
required_providers {
aws = {
source = "hashicorp/aws"
version = "~> 4.0"
}
}
}
provider "aws" {
region = var.aws_region
}
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
bucket = var.bucket_name
tags = {
Name = "My DevOps Book Bucket"
ManagedBy = "Terraform"
}
}
There are several ways to provide a value for bucket_name. One common way is to
create a file named terraform.tfvars. Terraform automatically loads any .tfvars files
it finds:
terraform.tfvars
bucket_name = "my-super-unique-bucket-from-a-variable"
Now, if you run terraform plan or terraform apply, Terraform will automatically use
the values from your .tfvars file. This allows you to easily use the same main.tf code
to create different buckets in different environments just by changing the .tfvars file.
